---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
---

<SiteLayout title="Notes" description="What we tested. What we didn't.">
  <div class="report-page">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>Notes</h1>
      <p class="page-note">What we tested. What we didn't.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="report-wrap">
      <article class="sheet report" aria-labelledby="report-title">
        <div class="sheet-content">
          <h2 class="report-heading" id="report-title">
            # Notes
          </h2>
          <p class="applies">
            Applies when someone asks how we know any of this works.
          </p>

          <section class="report-section" aria-labelledby="no-model">
            <h3 id="no-model">Why no model touches your writing</h3>
            <p>
              Milton stores your rules exactly as sent and your samples exactly
              as approved. Reading them back is a database query, with no model
              call. Your assistant does the writing. Milton keeps the file.
            </p>
            <p>We tested adding one. See below.</p>
          </section>

          <section class="report-section" aria-labelledby="sample-ranking">
            <h3 id="sample-ranking">How Milton picks eight samples</h3>
            <p>
              Milton keeps every sample you approve and serves eight per report.
              Your assistant says what it is writing, to whom, and why. Milton
              ranks your samples by who they were for and what they were for,
              then by matching words, then by date.
            </p>
            <p>
              We tested that against two alternatives: the newest eight, and
              Google's embedding model, which matches by meaning. We ran it
              twice, on fictional writers, with drafts scored blind:
            </p>
            <ul class="instruction-list">
              <li>
                <strong>Round 1:</strong> one writer, 150 samples, 20 drafted
                situations.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Round 2:</strong> three writers, 1,500 samples, 120
                drafted situations.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <table class="report-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Round 2</th>
                  <th scope="col">Found the right samples, 0 to 1</th>
                  <th scope="col">Draft score, out of 5</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Newest eight</th>
                  <td>0.32</td>
                  <td>3.77</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Milton's ranking</th>
                  <td>0.70</td>
                  <td>3.72</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Embeddings plus Milton's ranking</th>
                  <td>0.73</td>
                  <td>3.68</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>

            <p>
              Embeddings found slightly better matches and wrote no better
              drafts. They would also send your samples to a third party on
              every read. We left them out.
            </p>
            <p>
              The result we didn't expect:{" "}
              <strong>
                finding better samples did not produce better drafts.
              </strong>{" "}
              Our fictional writers wrote the same way to everyone, so any eight
              of their samples sounded alike. Real people write differently to a
              client than to a friend, which is where picking the right sample
              should matter. That test needs real writing, and it is next.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section class="report-section" aria-labelledby="sample-count">
            <h3 id="sample-count">How many samples</h3>
            <p>
              Real writing teaches an assistant more than nothing. In one study,
              five real emails roughly doubled how often a model's email could
              be traced to the person it was imitating, from 29% to 60%, and two
              emails did about as well as ten. That is why setup asks for one
              email first, and why your samples outrank the setup choices.
            </p>
            <p>
              In our own tests, cutting from eight samples to four made drafts
              clearly worse, 3.21 against 3.65 out of 5. Milton serves eight.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section class="report-section" aria-labelledby="untested">
            <h3 id="untested">What we haven't tested yet</h3>
            <ul class="instruction-list">
              <li>Drafts for real people, judged by those people.</li>
              <li>
                Assistants other than Claude Code. ChatGPT, Codex, and the rest
                are untested.
              </li>
              <li>Whether one short email is enough.</li>
              <li>
                Whether the setup choices, or any written rules, add anything
                beyond a pasted sample.
              </li>
              <li>Whether you can pick out your own writing, blind.</li>
            </ul>
          </section>

          <section class="report-section" aria-labelledby="test-method">
            <h3 id="test-method">How these tests were run</h3>
            <ul class="instruction-list">
              <li>
                The fictional writers and their samples were written by a model.
              </li>
              <li>
                Drafts were scored blind by a second model, twice each, against
                a description of the writer.
              </li>
              <li>
                No person has scored them yet. People are generous judges of
                their own voice: in one study, they rated drafts they had edited
                as just as much their own as text they wrote alone, though the
                drafts were measurably closer to the model's. So a person's
                score needs to be blind too.
              </li>
              <li>We set the pass mark before each run.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              Full notes:{" "}
              <a href="https://github.com/zoneofdanger/milton/blob/main/docs/research/sample-ranking-2026.md">
                Sample ranking
              </a>{" "}
              and{" "}
              <a href="https://github.com/zoneofdanger/milton/blob/main/docs/research/writing-evidence-2026.md">
                What the evidence supports
              </a>
              .
            </p>
            <p>
              Sources: Wang et al. (2025),{" "}
              <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.14543v1">
                Catch Me If You Can? Not Yet
              </a>
              ; Baumler et al. (2026),{" "}
              <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2604.24444">
                Can You Make It Sound Like You?
              </a>
              .
            </p>
          </section>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
